gm-method (auditing a heavily-pre-optimized vendored sibling repo for remaining perf cost): when a profile shows a sibling library's hot-path files at low single-digit percent of a CPU-bound frame, and its own git log shows continuous dedicated perf commits with matching AGENTS.md/recall history of 5+ prior audit batches, read the CURRENT full source fresh (not just recall summaries) before assuming stale wins remain -- prior sessions' declined-with-evidence findings (e.g. a ragged CSR-like structure flagged too risky to refactor without live rendering verification) stay declined unless new evidence changes the risk calculus. A vendored node_modules copy vs sibling-repo source diff showing large line deltas can be a CRLF-vs-LF false alarm -- normalize line endings (tr -d '' or dos2unix) before concluding a sync bug; check byte-identical-after-normalization before filing a sync finding. A file imported only by a standalone stress-test harness or referenced solely in a comment (not imported by the actual runtime entry point) is correctly dead-per-runtime-path, not a live cost, even if it still exists in the repo -- confirm via grep for actual import sites in the real entry file, not just file existence.